WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:05.770
It took decades, but fans of the 1982 cult
hit Tron finally got a sequel with Tron: Legacy

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:07.460
in 2010.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:13.070
Not long after, Disney announced a third movie,
code named Tron: Ascension, was in the works.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:18.119
Then… nothing, and the studio reportedly
pulled the plug in 2015.

00:00:18.119 --> 00:00:22.660
At this point, it seems Disney might be waiting
another three decades before considering its

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:23.710
next Tron movie.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:29.079
Here’s a look at why there won't be another
light cycle trip around the Grid anytime soon.

00:00:29.079 --> 00:00:30.079
Box office dud

00:00:30.079 --> 00:00:36.230
Tron: Legacy earned $400 million dollars worldwide
on a $170 million dollar budget.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:40.600
And while that's a lot of money for the sequel
to a 30-year-old cult hit, it still wasn't

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.060
quite enough to launch a franchise.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:47.030
For the sake of perspective, Disney’s live
action Alice in Wonderland was released in

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:51.720
the same year as Tron: Legacy and it brought
in more than $1 billion dollars at the box

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:52.720
office.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.370
$400 million dollars might sound like a lot,
but Disney is used to making a lot more.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.030
Not Disney enough

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:05.170
When the Tron sequel was axed in 2015, Disney's
release calendar was already booked pretty

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:10.850
solid with tentpole releases like Cinderella,
The Jungle Book, Beauty and the Beast, and,

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:12.869
yes, another Alice in Wonderland.

00:01:12.869 --> 00:01:14.450
What's the common theme here?

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:19.380
Outside of the Marvel movies, Disney's live
action slate is almost exclusively remakes

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:21.439
of its animated hits.

00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:25.679
Disney knows what works, and going with a
proven story that fans already love is far

00:01:25.679 --> 00:01:28.499
less risky that rolling the dice on another
Tron.

00:01:28.499 --> 00:01:33.039
Unless, of course, they can find a way to
shoehorn Iron Man into there...

00:01:33.039 --> 00:01:34.039
"Nope!"

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.749
Tomorrowland bombed

00:01:38.749 --> 00:01:44.779
Disney pulled the plug on a third Tron movie
in May 2015, almost immediately after Tomorrowland

00:01:44.779 --> 00:01:46.670
tanked at the box office.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:51.810
Like Tron, Tomorrowland was an ambitious sci-fi
film with a family-friendly angle.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:54.260
And like Tron, it didn’t do all that well.

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:59.369
Despite having George Clooney star and Brad
Bird in the director's chair, Tomorrowland

00:01:59.369 --> 00:02:04.009
couldn't clear $100 million dollars at the
U.S. box office — falling well below its

00:02:04.009 --> 00:02:06.270
reported $200 million budget.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:11.190
Fresh off Tron: Legacy’s lackluster performance,
Tomorrowland was confirmation that audiences

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:15.070
just aren’t interested enough in this type
of movie.

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:16.320
Took too long

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:20.670
The plot of the third Tron was reportedly
going to follow Olivia Wilde’s Quorra as

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:22.880
she ventures out into the real world.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:27.470
Wilde, co-star Garrett Hedlund, and director
Joseph Kosinski were all set to return.

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:31.350
But, when filming was about to start in late
2015, Disney shelved the project.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:35.640
At that point, original star Bruce Boxleitner
said he was no longer interested in making

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.500
another Tron, after seeing how Disney was
treating the franchise.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:39.930
"I’m done with it.

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:40.930
I’ve moved on.

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:43.920
I hate to say that but it’s been too up
and down for me."

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.570
And if they did move forward now, it’d be
pretty hard to make a Tron movie without Tron.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:50.760
Tron: Uprising crashed

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:57.330
In 2012, Disney XD launched the animated series
Tron: Uprising to tie into the world of Tron:

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.660
Legacy and potentially set up a new live action
sequel.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:04.840
The show was actually pretty good, but after
launching with nearly 2 million viewers, the

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:09.420
ratings took a nose dive to around 300,000
by the time the first season wrapped.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:14.030
If Disney was looking to the series as a proving
ground that fans wanted more Tron, it pretty

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:15.920
much proved the opposite.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:17.920
"Difficult proposition.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:20.180
Difficult proposition at best."

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.050
Toy troubles

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:26.180
Disney makes a lot of movies, and it also
sells a whole lot of toys.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:31.770
A lot of tie-in merchandise was launched alongside
Tron: Legacy in 2010, but the toys and games

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:36.730
were never really big sellers, which closed
off a potentially huge financial avenue for

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:38.360
Disney and its partners.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:43.180
The Tron: Evolution video game, which served
as a prequel to Tron: Legacy, sold a mere

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:48.360
190,000 copies — which is pretty terrible
for a hyped game built around what should've

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.340
been a hot property.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:51.340
Waiting game

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:54.620
The time might not be right for a new Tron
sequel now.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:57.250
But what’s old will be new again eventually,
right?

00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:01.960
Franchises like these tend to come back around
after a while in Hollywood, and it stands

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:04.460
to reason Tron will be no exception.

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:08.330
If it ever does, it sounds like they at least
have a great script ready to go.

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:12.520
"Yeah, between us, they were like, 'This one's
going to blow Legacy out of the water.'"

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:16.820
So, if it took 30 years for Tron: Legacy to
get made, we can go ahead and pencil in a

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:19.760
date around 2040 for the next one, right?

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:21.060
Thanks for watching!

